<book id="kwin-minimize2tray" lang="&language;">
<bookinfo>
<title>The Minimize2Tray Handbook</title>
<date>2026-11-14</date><releaseinfo>2.4.2</releaseinfo>
<abstract><para>Window rules and tray controls for the PhobosK fork of KWin Minimize2Tray.</para></abstract>
<keywordset><keyword>KDE</keyword><keyword>KWin</keyword><keyword>Minimize2Tray</keyword></keywordset>
</bookinfo>

<chapter id="introduction"><title>Getting started</title>
<para>Enable <guilabel>Minimize2Tray</guilabel> on the <guilabel>KWin Scripts</guilabel> page in System Settings. The script requires its native tray plugin and configuration module.</para>
<para>Native Wayland and X11 windows are supported. Applications may expose different identifying properties in each session type, so use <guibutton>Detect window properties</guibutton> and the preview <emphasis>in the session where you will use the rule</emphasis>.</para>

<sect1 id="tray-controls"><title>Pinning and controlling a window</title>
<para>Press <keycombo action="simul">&Meta;&Alt;<keycap>Page Down</keycap></keycombo> to pin and hide the active main window. Left-click its tray icon to show or hide that window on its assigned activity and virtual desktop.</para>
<note><title>One icon, one window</title>
<para>A tray icon belongs to <emphasis role="bold">one window</emphasis>, rather than every window of the application. Hidden windows normally leave the taskbar, switcher and pager; an attention request may expose a window on the taskbar.</para>
</note>
<para>Right-click an icon for these actions, in menu order:</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>Show/Hide</term><listitem><para>Restore the window or hide it in the tray. Showing a window prevents startup hiding from running again for it; later minimize or optional close actions can still hide it.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Unpin</term><listitem><para>Show the window and remove its tray icon. Automatic actions stay suppressed until no enabled rule matches or you manually pin the window again.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Add tray rule…</term><listitem><para>Pick a window and create or review its automatic tray rule. See <xref linkend="creating-rules"/>.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Configure…</term><listitem><para>Open Minimize2Tray settings and rules for all applications.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>About</term><listitem><para>View the version, upstream authorship and fork links.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Quit</term><listitem><para>Close the associated window normally. Other windows of that application may remain open.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="shortcuts"><title>Keyboard shortcuts</title>
<para>In <guilabel>System Settings</guilabel>, open <guilabel>Shortcuts</guilabel> and search for Minimize2Tray's actions.</para>
<informaltable><tgroup cols="2"><thead><row><entry>Action</entry><entry>Default shortcut</entry></row></thead><tbody>
<row><entry>Minimize to tray</entry><entry><keycombo action="simul">&Meta;&Alt;<keycap>Page Down</keycap></keycombo></entry></row>
<row><entry>Add tray rule</entry><entry>Unassigned</entry></row>
<row><entry>Close or hide window to tray</entry><entry>Unassigned</entry></row>
</tbody></tgroup></informaltable>
<para>KDE manages shortcut assignments separately. They are <emphasis>not included</emphasis> in Minimize2Tray configuration backups.</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<chapter id="rules"><title>Window rules</title>
<para><emphasis role="bold">Choose which windows match, then choose what happens to them.</emphasis> Rules can pin a window, start it hidden, hide it when minimized, or use an optional close shortcut.</para>
<tip><title>Find the help you need</title>
<para>Start with <link linkend="creating-rules">Creating and reviewing rules</link>, check <link linkend="matching">Matching properties</link>, then choose your <link linkend="actions">Rule actions</link>. The <link linkend="preview-help">preview and inline help</link> explain the editor's unsaved settings.</para>
</tip>
<sect1 id="creating-rules"><title>Creating and reviewing rules</title>
<para><guibutton>Add rule…</guibutton> opens a manual editor. To generate a rule from a window, use <guimenuitem>Add tray rule…</guimenuitem> from any managed tray icon or its assigned shortcut:</para>
<orderedlist>
<listitem><para>Select the desired application window with KWin's native crosshair picker. Press <keycap>Escape</keycap> to cancel.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>If a rule already matches, choose <guibutton>Edit existing rule</guibutton>, <guibutton>Create a narrower rule</guibutton> or <guibutton>Cancel</guibutton>.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>For a new rule, Minimize2Tray saves the rule and hides the selected window. Choose <guibutton>Review rule…</guibutton> to inspect it, or <guibutton>Keep rule</guibutton> to finish without another dialog.</para></listitem>
</orderedlist>
<important><title>Automatic rules are saved immediately</title>
<para>A generated rule explicitly enables <guilabel>Start in tray</guilabel> and <guilabel>Minimize to tray: On</guilabel>. It hides the selected window now and applies those actions to future matches.</para>
</important>
<para>Automatic rules use available application identity, class, instance, role and type. An exact title narrows the selection when needed. Indistinguishable windows are rejected rather than guessed.</para>
<tip><title>Prefer stable properties</title>
<para>A window role is usually more stable than a title. An exact title stops matching when the title changes. A narrower rule does <emphasis>not disable</emphasis> the wider rule: both rules' enabled actions still combine.</para>
</tip>

<para><emphasis role="bold">Existing values are never replaced by suggestions.</emphasis> Colored suggestions remain inactive until you select <guilabel>Use</guilabel>. The <guilabel>Automatically created</guilabel> label identifies a rule kept as generated, including one reviewed without changes. Saving a change removes the label; reverting edits before saving preserves it.</para>
<para>A rule's name and description organize the list; they do not match window titles. <guilabel>Enable this rule</guilabel> controls whether its conditions and actions apply. Search filters only the visible list.</para>
<para><guilabel>Sort by</guilabel> orders the list by name or creation time without changing stored rule order. Older rules may have unknown creation dates. <guibutton>Delete</guibutton> and <guibutton>Undo delete</guibutton> affect the pending list until Apply or OK saves it.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="matching"><title>Matching properties</title>
<note><title>All checked conditions must match</title>
<para>Every selected <guilabel>Use</guilabel> condition must match the same window. Separate enabled rules combine their actions.</para>
</note>
<para><guibutton>Detect window properties…</guibutton> reads a window using KWin's picker, then lets you choose values to copy. <guilabel>Delay</guilabel> gives you time to open the desired window.</para>
<para>Related configuration windows are hidden during picking and restored afterwards. With the script disabled, only this configuration process can be hidden. Configuration and system windows remain protected as targets.</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>Application ID</term><listitem>
<para>The launcher ID, optionally ending in <literal>.desktop</literal> for an exact match. When a window omits a usable ID, unique installed desktop metadata can associate its class or executable name with a launcher. Ambiguous associations stay unresolved.</para>
<para>Starting an application through systemd does not require changing its class to the desktop filename. Use the class obtained by Detect or choose an explicit icon instead of inventing an Application ID.</para>
</listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Window class, instance and whole class</term><listitem>
<para>These are application-reported identifiers. <guilabel>Whole window class</guilabel> matches the instance, one space, then the class, as in KWin rules. An instance is not necessarily unique to one window.</para>
<para>Use Detect for the actual values. Imported <guilabel>Class or instance</guilabel> conditions match either identifier.</para>
</listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Window role and title</term><listitem><para>A role may distinguish an editor from the main window. Titles often change with files or pages. Prefer stable properties and inspect the preview before applying.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Window type and child protection</term><listitem>
<para>Choose normal windows, utility windows, non-modal dialogs or all supported types. <guilabel>Keep child windows and dialogs visible</guilabel> excludes children even when their type is selected.</para>
<para>Modal dialogs, system surfaces, Minimize2Tray, KWin and configuration hosts <emphasis role="bold">always remain protected</emphasis>.</para>
</listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<sect2 id="match-modes"><title>Match modes</title>
<informaltable><tgroup cols="2"><thead><row><entry>Mode</entry><entry>Meaning</entry></row></thead><tbody>
<row><entry>Exact match</entry><entry>Compare the complete value.</entry></row>
<row><entry>Substring match</entry><entry>Find a literal part of the value.</entry></row>
<row><entry>Regular expression</entry><entry>Search using a PCRE2 expression. Use <literal>^</literal> and <literal>$</literal> to require the complete value.</entry></row>
</tbody></tgroup></informaltable>
<para>All modes are <emphasis role="bold">case-sensitive</emphasis>. Invalid regular expressions cannot be saved. Expression execution is bounded, and values longer than 4096 characters are truncated for expression matching. An expression exceeding its execution limit does not match. Avoid broad patterns that target unrelated applications.</para>

</sect2>
<sect2 id="preview-help"><title>Preview and inline help</title>
<para><guilabel>Preview with open windows</guilabel> uses your <emphasis>unsaved edits</emphasis>. Click its column headings to sort by Window or Result; the default is Window, ascending. <guibutton>Refresh</guibutton> obtains current properties. The script must be enabled to verify eligibility.</para>
<para>Small circled information icons beside selected labels and Match dropdowns repeat the corresponding tooltip on hover, including for disabled options. The pointer becomes a help cursor. Use and Value cells have no additional icons; their existing hover tooltips still work.</para>
<tip><title>Scroll without changing your rule</title>
<para>Scrolling over a closed Match dropdown scrolls the editor without changing the match mode. Open the dropdown or use its keyboard controls to make an intentional selection.</para>
</tip>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="actions"><title>Rule actions</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>Startup</term><listitem>
<para><guilabel>Use global startup</guilabel> follows the <guilabel>Tray &amp; badges</guilabel> page. Existing rules retain their previous explicit startup choices when upgraded.</para>
<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para><guilabel>No startup action</guilabel>: leave the window alone.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Pin without initially hiding</guilabel>: add an icon while preserving visibility.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para><guilabel>Start in tray</guilabel>: create an icon and hide the window when it first matches, including existing windows when rules are applied.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Retry early startup restoration once</term><listitem>
<para>If an application immediately restores itself, retry hiding once within 1.5 seconds. Activation, Show and Unpin cancel the retry. A restore without activation can also be retried, so leave this off if it interferes.</para>
<para>This takes effect only when the <emphasis>effective</emphasis> startup action is <guilabel>Start in tray</guilabel>.</para>
</listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Minimize to tray</term><listitem><para><guilabel>On</guilabel> hides minimized matches in the tray. <guilabel>Off</guilabel> preserves normal minimization. <guilabel>Use global</guilabel> follows Hide on minimize. This works independently of the startup choice.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Close to tray through the optional shortcut</term><listitem>
<para>Assign <guilabel>Close or hide window to tray</guilabel> separately in KDE Shortcuts. It hides matching windows when enabled; otherwise that shortcut closes the window normally.</para>
<para>It does <emphasis role="bold">not intercept</emphasis> titlebar Close, <keycombo action="simul">&Alt;<keycap>F4</keycap></keycombo> or application Quit.</para>
</listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Quit on logout</term><listitem>
<para>An explicit opt-in to send <literal>SIGTERM</literal> to a verified native Wayland application's process during logout, restart or shutdown if KDE has disabled closing its matching window.</para>
<warning><title>This may end the entire application process</title>
<para>The process may terminate <emphasis role="bold">without save prompts</emphasis>. Cancelling logout cannot recover a terminated application.</para>
</warning>
<para>Requires Linux 6.13 or later and an adapter built for the running KWin version; rebuild after KWin updates. XWayland, X11, unverifiable and protected processes are left alone. An unavailable build lets you turn an existing opt-in off.</para>
</listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Override the tray icon</term><listitem>
<para><emphasis role="bold">Off by default.</emphasis> Choose a local desktop file or an image supported by Qt. A desktop file supplies only its <literal>Icon</literal> value; its commands are never executed.</para>
<para>An enabled override replaces all automatic icon detection and affects <emphasis>presentation only</emphasis>, not matching or badges. Keep the file available; a missing icon produces a generic fallback.</para>
<para>The first enabled override in stored rule order wins when rules overlap. Sorting the visible list does not change that order.</para>
</listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<sect2 id="overlapping-rules"><title>When several rules match</title>
<para>Start in tray wins over pin-only. Minimize On wins over Off, then the global default. Any matching rule can enable Close to tray, startup retry or the explicit logout action. Removing the last automatic action restores state owned by that action; manual pins remain independent.</para>
<important><title>Editor OK is not the final save</title>
<para><guibutton>OK</guibutton> in the editor updates the pending rule list; <guibutton>Cancel</guibutton> discards editor changes. <emphasis role="bold">Apply or OK on the main page</emphasis> saves the configuration and updates running windows.</para>
<para><guibutton>Reload settings</guibutton> discards pending edits when another configuration window has saved.</para>
</important>
</sect2>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<chapter id="tray"><title>Tray and badges</title>
<sect1 id="tray-defaults"><title>Default window actions</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>Startup</term><listitem><para>The default for rules choosing <guilabel>Use global startup</guilabel>. Its initial value is <guilabel>Start in tray</guilabel>. It never targets applications without a matching rule, and existing rules keep their explicit startup behavior.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Hide on minimize</term><listitem><para>The default for inheriting rules and manually pinned windows without matching rules. Per-rule On and Off override it.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>
<sect1 id="badges"><title>Counters, progress and attention</title>
<para>Applications may publish counters, progress and urgency through Unity LauncherAPI. Minimize2Tray displays the data they publish; <emphasis>notifications do not create unread counts</emphasis>.</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>Counter as dot</term><listitem><para>Replace a positive number with a dot.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>Counter display limit</term><listitem><para>Cap the displayed number, for example <literal>99+</literal>, without changing the application's count.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>Progress and attention can overlay the icon. Availability depends on the application and tray host.</para>
</sect1>
<sect1 id="tray-icons"><title>Where icons come from</title>
<para>Icons normally come from the application's installed desktop file, discovered by its reported ID or an unambiguous class/executable association. Otherwise the window icon is retained. User desktop-file overrides in the standard applications directories are honored.</para>
<tip><title>Choose an icon without changing matching</title>
<para>A per-rule <guilabel>Override the tray icon</guilabel> has priority over every automatic source. Do not add an unrelated Application ID solely to choose an icon.</para>
</tip>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<chapter id="advanced"><title>Backups and troubleshooting</title>
<sect1 id="troubleshooting"><title>Diagnosing unexpected behavior</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry><term>A window is not hidden</term><listitem><para>Use Detect and Preview to check actual properties, all checked conditions, window type and child protection. On Wayland, do not use X11-only tools as evidence for a native window.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>The tray icon is generic</term><listitem><para>Check the installed launcher or select a per-rule icon override. Do not add an unrelated Application ID to select an icon.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
<varlistentry><term>More detail is needed</term><listitem><para><guilabel>Enable debug logging</guilabel> writes diagnostic messages to KWin's logs. Leave it off during normal use.</para></listitem></varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>
<sect1 id="backups"><title>Exporting and importing configuration</title>
<para><guibutton>View as text…</guibutton> displays read-only JSON, including pending and disabled rules. <guibutton>Export configuration…</guibutton> saves all rules, options and sorting to a JSON backup.</para>
<orderedlist>
<listitem><para>Choose <guibutton>Import configuration…</guibutton> and select a backup.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Select rules, options or both. Merge rules into the current list or replace them. Adding skips identical copies and repairs conflicting rule IDs.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>Review the pending result, then choose Apply or OK to save. Unselected items remain unchanged.</para></listitem>
</orderedlist>
<note><title>What a backup does not include</title>
<para>Shortcuts and unrelated KWin settings are excluded. Imported icon paths must be available locally; the image files themselves are not embedded in the backup.</para>
</note>
<para>Older rule formats are migrated while preserving explicit behavior, with a backup made when saved. A creation date that was not recorded remains unknown. Invalid changes are reported, and runtime reload keeps the last valid configuration.</para>
<para>Simultaneous saves are guarded. If another window has saved settings, reload rather than overwriting its changes.</para>
</sect1>
<sect1 id="restarts"><title>Disabling, restarting and updating</title>
<para>Disabling the script restores managed windows and removes its tray items. KWin or Plasma restarts can temporarily interrupt tray availability.</para>
<important><title>Native plugin updates need a fresh load</title>
<para>After updating native code, a new KWin session may be needed. Live rule reload does <emphasis>not replace a library already loaded in KWin</emphasis>.</para>
</important>
<para>Build and installation instructions, distribution packages and project issue links are in the repository README.</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>
</book>
